    "summary": "High‑energy first set with scorching Loser solo; second set loses steam",
    "blurb": "Feel Like A Stranger and Loser launch a tight, high‑octane first set that stays loose through Lazy River Road. The second half stalls – Box of Rain and He's Gone feel tired while Wharf Rat provides the only real lift.",
    "review": "March 29, 1993 at Albany’s Knickerbocker Arena delivered a quintessential early‑'90s Dead experience: the band burst out of the gate with a blistering Feel Like A Stranger and an incendiary Loser solo that reviewers called “the best solos of the era.” Jerry’s lead guitar was on fire, and Bill Kreutzmann supplied what many fans described as “amazing” drumming throughout. The momentum stayed high through Lazy River Road and even a rollicking Johnny B. Goode, keeping the first set tight and exhilarating.\n\nWhen the second set rolled in, the energy dipped. A rain‑soaked atmosphere surrounded Box of Rain and He's Gone, but both were noted as under‑performed, with Jerry’s vocals sagging and Bob sounding tired, especially on Throwing Stones. The Drum/Space segment was solid, yet the subsequent songs never recaptured the first‑set spark, only salvaged by a soulful Wharf Rat before closing with Not Fade Away and an encore Liberty.",
